}{\plain \fs16 \b\f4\fs16 VII.  Of the Old Testament.}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
}\pard \fs16\sl120\slmult1\tx0\tx306\tx1116\tx2286\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640 
{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
}\pard \fs16\tx0\tx306\tx1116\tx2286\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640
{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 The Old Testament is not contrary to the New: for both in the Old and New Testament \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 everlasting life is offered to Mankind by Christ, who is the only Mediator between God and \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 Man, being both God and Man. Wherefore they are not to be heard, which feign that the \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 old Fathers did look only for transitory promises.  Although the Law given from God by\par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 Moses, as touching Ceremonies and Rites, do not bind Christian men, nor the Civil precepts\par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 thereof ought of necessity to be received in any commonwealth; yet notwithstanding, no \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 Christian man whatsoever is free from the obedience of the Commandments which are \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 called Moral.\par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
