{\plain \fs36 \f4\fs36 Tables and Rules for Finding \par
}{\plain \fs36 \f4\fs36 the Date of Easter Day}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
}\pard \fs16\tx0\tx306\tx1116\tx3096\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640
{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \b\f4\fs20 Rules for Finding the Date of Easter Day}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
}\pard \fs16\sl120\slmult1\tx0\tx306\tx1116\tx3096\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640 
{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
}\pard \fs16\tx0\tx306\tx1116\tx3096\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640
{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 Easter Day is always the Sunday after the full moon that occurs on or after the spring \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 equinox on March 21, a date which is fixed in accordance with an ancient ecclesiastical \softline
computation, and which does not always correspond to the astronomical equinox. This full \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 moon may happen on any date between March 21 and April 18 inclusive. If the full moon\par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 falls on a Sunday, Easter Day is the Sunday following. But Easter Day cannot be earlier \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 than March 22 or later than April 25.\par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 To find the date of Easter Day in any particular year, it is necessary to have two points of \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 reference {\u8212\'97} the Golden Number and the Sunday Letter for that year.\par
}\pard \fs16\sl120\slmult1\tx0\tx306\tx1116\tx3096\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640 
{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
}\pard \fs16\tx0\tx306\tx1116\tx3096\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640
{\plain \fs16 \b\f4\fs16 1.  The Golden Number}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16  indicates the date of the full moon on or after the spring equinox of \softline
March 21, according to a nineteen-year cycle. These Numbers are prefixed in the Calendar \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 to the days of the month from March 22 to April 18 inclusive. In the present Calendar they \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 are applicable from A.D. 1900 to A.D. 2099, after which they will change.\par
}\pard \fs16\sl120\slmult1\tx0\tx306\tx1116\tx3096\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640 
{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
}\pard \fs16\tx0\tx306\tx1116\tx3096\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640
{\plain \fs16 \b\f4\fs16 2.  The Sunday Letter}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16  identifies the days of the year when Sundays occur. After every date in \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 the Calendar a letter appears {\u8212\'97} from A to g. Thus, if January 1 is a Sunday, the Sunday \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 Letter for the year is A, and every date in the Calendar marked by A is a Sunday. If January \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 2 is a Sunday, then every date marked with b is a Sunday, and so on through the seven letters.\par
}\pard \fs16\sl120\slmult1\tx0\tx306\tx1116\tx3096\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640 
{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
}\pard \fs16\tx0\tx306\tx1116\tx3096\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640
{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 In Leap Years, however, the Sunday Letter changes on the first day of March. In such years, \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 when A is the Sunday Letter, this applies only to Sundays in January and February, and g is \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 the Sunday Letter for the rest of the year. Or if d is the Sunday Letter, then c is the Sunday \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 Letter on and after March 1.\par
}\pard \fs16\sl360\slmult1\tx0\tx306\tx1116\tx3096\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640 
{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
}\pard \fs16\tx0\tx306\tx1116\tx3096\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640
{\plain \fs16 \b\f4\fs16 To Find the Golden Number}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
}\pard \fs16\sl120\slmult1\tx0\tx306\tx1116\tx3096\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640 
{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
}\pard \fs16\tx0\tx306\tx1116\tx3096\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640
{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 The Golden Number of any year is calculated as follows: Take the number of the year, add \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 1, and then divide the sum by 19.  The remainder, if any, is the Golden Number. If nothing \softline
remains, then 19 is the Golden Number.\par
}\pard \fs16\sl360\slmult1\tx0\tx306\tx1116\tx3096\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640 
{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
}\pard \fs16\tx0\tx306\tx1116\tx3096\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640
{\plain \fs16 \b\f4\fs16 To Find the Sunday Letter}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
}\pard \fs16\sl120\slmult1\tx0\tx306\tx1116\tx3096\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640 
{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
}\pard \fs16\tx0\tx306\tx1116\tx3096\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640
{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 The following Table provides ready reference to the Sunday Letter of any year between  A.D. \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 1900 and  A.D. 2099. It will be found on the line of the hundredth year above the column \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 that contains the remaining digits of the year. But in Leap Years the Letter above the \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 number marked with an asterisk is the Sunday Letter for January and February, and the\par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 Letter over the number not so marked is the Sunday Letter for the rest of the year.\par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
