{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 2.  Easter Day will be the next date bearing the Sunday Letter of that year. But when the \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 Golden Number of a given year and the Sunday Letter of that year occur on the same date, \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 then Easter day is one week later. (For example, if the Golden Number is 19 {\u8212\'97} which \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 appears in the Calendar prefixed to March 27 {\u8212\'97} and the Sunday Letter is d, then Easter Day \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 in that year will fall on March 29. If the Golden Number is 10 and the Sunday Letter is A, \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 then Easter Day will fall on April 9. But if the Golden Number is 19 and the Sunday Letter \par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 is b, then Easter Day will be one week later, namely April 3.)\par
}{\plain \fs16 \f4\fs16 \par
